--- !u!114 &29557
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 3646}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 1453722849, guid: 67dfb1fdfb2b407222eda8e23ac8b724, type: 3}
  m_Name:
  m_EditorClassIdentifier:
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "<size=35>1. Corruption Mechanism</size>\r\n• After state establishment, corruption value gradually increases over time\r\n• Corruption accumulation speed affected by:\r\n<indent=5%>- Population: High population (Malthusian trap) accelerates corruption\r\n- Economy: Treasury deficit accelerates corruption</indent>\r\n• High corruption triggers potential background reform event (no event text yet), resetting corruption value\r\n\r\n<size=35>2. Population System</size>\r\n• Provincial population cap based on current ecosystem\r\n• Population growth rate affected by ethnic attributes\r\n• Wars cause population decline\r\n• Low population reduces willingness to initiate wars\r\n• Population determines troop replenishment rate\r\n\r\n<size=35>3. Economy and Specialty System</size>\r\n• Provincial resources determined by ecosystem\r\n• Economic output affected by ethnic attributes and state system\r\n• Military expenses depend on troop mobilization\r\n• Administrative efficiency determined by territorial size\r\n• Taxation based on population and specialties\r\n• Wars reduce economic output to zero\r\n• Corruption affects tax efficiency\r\n• Treasury deficit reduces willingness to initiate wars\r\n\r\n<size=35>4. Core Area</size>\r\n• Expands over time from capital\r\n• Emergency mobilization when threatened\r\n• Mobilization scale affected by corruption and economy\r\n• Leaves national ethnic imprint, deepening with time\r\n• Removes extinct nations' imprints\r\n\r\n<size=35>5. Rebellion and Nation Restoration System</size>\r\n• Rebellion probability increases with distance from core\r\n• Remote areas prone to rebellion during high corruption\r\n• Rebellions may restore old nations in provinces\r\n• Extreme corruption may cause core area rebellions\r\n\r\n<size=35>6. Combat System</size>\r\n• Army effectiveness affected by terrain, economy, corruption, national traits\r\n• Internal operations: easier supply, lower expenses\r\n• Expeditions/overseas: higher consumption and expenses\r\n• Surrounded troops in \"salient\", halved effectiveness\r\n• Island strategy: Frequent harassment of continental nations\r\n<indent=5%>- May overextend continental armies</indent>\r\n• Distant overseas operations: maritime, commercial, or powerful nations only\r\n• Armies prioritize border deployment\r\n• Army types: garrison, imperial guards, field troops\r\n<indent=5%>- Field: foreign attacks\r\n- Guards: rebellion suppression\r\n- Garrison: stationing</indent>\r\n• Threatened capital improves army efficiency, changes troop roles\r\n• Fortresses: Mountain-built, hard to conquer\r\n\r\n<size=35>7. Diplomatic System</size>\r\n<size=30>Basic Political System Relations</size>\r\n<size=30>Republic/Federal</size>\r\n• Friendly: republics, federal systems\r\n• Good: kingdoms\r\n• Tense: tribal systems\r\n• Opposed: empires, theocracies\r\n\r\n<size=30>Empire</size>\r\n• Close: kingdoms\r\n• Neutral: tribal, theocratic\r\n• Tense: republics, federal\r\n• Competitive: other empires\r\n\r\n<size=30>Kingdom</size>\r\n• Good: republics, federal, other kingdoms\r\n• Slightly good: theocracies\r\n• Neutral: tribal\r\n• Slightly tense: empires\r\n\r\n<size=30>Tribal</size>\r\n• Close: other tribes\r\n• Neutral: kingdoms, theocracies\r\n• Tense: republics, federal, empires\r\n\r\n<size=30>Theocracy</size>\r\n• Good: kingdoms\r\n• Neutral: tribal\r\n• Tense: republics, federal, empires\r\n• Potential conflicts: other theocracies\r\n• Special: Strong religious propagation\r\n\r\n<size=35>National Power and Diplomacy</size>\r\n1. Strong Country Effect:\r\n<indent=5%>• Overly powerful countries face deteriorating relations</indent>\r\n2. Corruption and War Risk:\r\n<indent=5%>• Powerful but corrupt countries vulnerable to attacks</indent>\r\n\r\n<size=35>Dynamic Diplomacy</size>\r\n• Relations change based on: common enemies, history, power, corruption\r\n• Theocracies: strong religious conversion\r\n• Overly powerful allies may dissolve alliances\r\n\r\n<size=35>Randomness</size>\r\n• Systems not absolute, random factors simulate low-probability events"
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: d7e6e81d6d5c86245b4577d7d5302710, type: 2}
  m_sharedMaterial: {fileID: 2100000, guid: a1d694cf08390b44b9cfc4d8c31013b9, type: 2}
